\newcommand{\PropForm}{\mathsf{Form}}
\newcommand{\Ord}{\mathsf{Ord}}
\newcommand{\ctx}{\Gamma}

\title{Guarded Circular Proof Graphs:\\Ordinal Descent, Soundness, and the Limits of Self-Reference}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{17 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Proofs are conventionally represented by well-founded trees, whereas recursive programs, protocol reductions, and cyclic derivations are naturally represented by graphs. We study a minimal implication calculus in which proof graphs may have arbitrary node types and dependency structure, while validity is controlled by an ordinal-valued progress certificate. A graph is locally well typed when each node satisfies its indicated assumption or implication-introduction rule, and it is guarded when ordinal rank strictly decreases along every dependency edge. The central soundness theorem states that every guarded, locally well-typed proof graph unfolds nodewise into an ordinary natural-deduction derivation.

Several boundary results sharpen this statement. The standard proof of $P\to P$ is a height-one, two-node derivation with a rank-$1$ implication node and a rank-$0$ assumption leaf; it is not genuinely circular. No direct self-loop admits a decreasing ordinal rank and, more generally, strict descent forbids every nonempty finite dependency cycle. Edgewise ordinal guarding therefore validates graph compression and apparent back-reference, but cannot validate genuinely cyclic justification. A complementary diagonal result explains why, in a sound self-referential system, a sentence asserting its own unprovability remains unprovable. We conclude with certificate-checking algorithms, applications to recursive security arguments, and directions involving Scott domains, trace conditions, and guarded modalities.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Self-reference occupies an ambiguous place in logic. Recursion is indispensable: functions call themselves on smaller inputs, infinite streams reveal one constructor before continuing, and inductive arguments invoke hypotheses at lower complexity. Yet the inference ``$P$ holds because this proof of $P$ says so'' is empty. Both productive recursion and vicious circularity contain a backward reference; the difference is progress.

We isolate that difference in a small setting. The language contains atoms and implication. Ordinary derivations use assumptions and implication introduction. We enlarge the syntax from finite proof trees to proof graphs whose nodes carry local instructions and may have arbitrary dependencies. We then impose a global ordinal ranking: every dependency leads to a node of strictly smaller rank.

This approach separates graph syntax, local correctness, and global justification. Local rule compliance does not itself make a cyclic diagram sound. Conversely, the familiar proof of $P\to P$ is not circular merely because it temporarily assumes $P$: the assumption occurs in an enlarged local context and is discharged.

Our principal result is conservative. Ordinal-guarded graphs yield nothing beyond ordinary derivations. This is useful because graphs can share repeated structure and express recursive organization, but it is also a limitation: strict descent on every edge excludes every genuine finite cycle. A semantics for truly cyclic proofs must weaken edgewise descent while retaining global progress.

The cryptographic relevance lies in recursive reductions. Security arguments are often organized as graphs of games and adversary transformations. A reduction returning to the same claim without lowering a well-founded measure is no argument. A rank certificate can make progress explicit through remaining rounds, protocol phases, syntactic complexity, or a lexicographic combination.

\section{Implication logic and ordinary derivations}

Fix an arbitrary set $\mathcal A$ of atomic propositions.

\begin{definition}[Formulas]
The set $\PropForm(\mathcal A)$ is inductively generated as follows: every $a\in\mathcal A$ gives an atomic formula, and if $A,B\in\PropForm(\mathcal A)$, then $A\to B\in\PropForm(\mathcal A)$. A context $\ctx$ is a finite list of formulas; $A::\ctx$ denotes adjoining $A$ at the front.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Ordinary derivability]
The judgment $\ctx\vdash A$ is generated by
\[
\frac{A\in\ctx}{\ctx\vdash A}\quad\text{(assumption)}
\qquad
\frac{A::\ctx\vdash B}{\ctx\vdash A\to B}\quad\text{(implication introduction).}
\]
\end{definition}

Derivability is inductively generated, so every ordinary derivation is a finite well-founded tree. The assumption rule is local: it concludes $A$ only where $A$ is available, not unconditionally.

\section{Proof graphs and ordinal guards}

Let $V$ be an arbitrary set of nodes. No finiteness or acyclicity assumption is imposed.

\begin{definition}[Local instructions]
A node carries either an \emph{assumption} instruction, requiring no child, or an \emph{implication-introduction} instruction containing formulas $A,B$ and a designated child $m\in V$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Proof graph]
A proof graph consists, for every $n\in V$, of a context $\ctx_n$, a conclusion $C_n$, and a local instruction $I_n$. At this stage the graph is only a candidate derivation diagram.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Local well-typedness]
A proof graph is locally well typed if each node satisfies the following conditions.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item If $I_n$ is assumption, then $C_n\in\ctx_n$.
\item If $I_n$ is implication introduction with formulas $A,B$ and child $m$, then
\[
C_n=A\to B,\qquad \ctx_m=A::\ctx_n,\qquad C_m=B.
\]
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

Local typing checks that each node resembles a legal rule application. It cannot guarantee that repeatedly following dependencies reaches evidence.

\begin{definition}[Ordinal guardedness]
An ordinal ranking is a map $\rho:V\to\Ord$. The graph is \emph{guarded by $\rho$} if every implication node $n$ with child $m$ satisfies
\[
\rho(m)<\rho(n).
\]
Assumption nodes have no dependency and impose no inequality.
\end{definition}

The rank is a progress certificate. An edge may point backward in a drawing, but it must point downward in logical rank.

\section{Soundness by well-founded unfolding}

\begin{theorem}[Guarded Graph Soundness]
Let a proof graph be locally well typed and guarded by an ordinal ranking $\rho$. Then every node $n$ has an ordinary derivation
\[
\ctx_n\vdash C_n.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Define $m\prec n$ when $\rho(m)<\rho(n)$. This relation is well founded because strict ordinal order is well founded. Proceed by well-founded induction on an arbitrary node $n$.

If $n$ is an assumption node, local typing gives $C_n\in\ctx_n$, and the assumption rule gives $\ctx_n\vdash C_n$.

If $n$ introduces an implication using formulas $A,B$ and child $m$, local typing gives
\[
C_n=A\to B,\qquad \ctx_m=A::\ctx_n,\qquad C_m=B.
\]
Guardedness gives $m\prec n$. The induction hypothesis therefore yields $A::\ctx_n\vdash B$. Implication introduction gives $\ctx_n\vdash A\to B$, which is the desired judgment. These exhaust the instructions.
\end{proof}

The theorem is a conservativity result: guarded graph presentations cannot prove anything unavailable to ordinary natural deduction. It also identifies the essential premise. Local typing supplies rule-specific facts, while well-founded descent licenses recursive use of child derivations.

The pattern extends to any finitary proof calculus. For a rule with children $m_1,\ldots,m_k$, require $\rho(m_i)<\rho(n)$ for every premise and require local typing to match the rule's premise sequents. The same induction reconstructs an ordinary derivation.

\section{The height-one identity}

Fix a formula $P$. Let $r$ be a root and $\ell$ a leaf. Give $r$ empty context, conclusion $P\to P$, and an implication-introduction instruction with child $\ell$. Give $\ell$ context $[P]$, conclusion $P$, and the assumption instruction. Set $\rho(r)=1$ and $\rho(\ell)=0$.

\begin{theorem}[Height-One Identity]
For every formula $P$, the two-node construction has root ordinal height $1$, leaf height $0$, and yields
\[
[]\vdash P\to P.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The leaf derives $P$ from $[P]$ by assumption. The root discharges that occurrence by implication introduction. Equivalently, the graph is locally typed, its sole dependency satisfies $0<1$, and Guarded Graph Soundness applies.
\end{proof}

The identity proof is therefore not self-referential. The leaf does not establish $P$ without conditions; it establishes $P$ under the temporary assumption $P$. The final result is $P\to P$, not $P$.

\section{Strict descent excludes cycles}

\begin{theorem}[No Guarded Self-Reference]
For every node $n$ and ordinal ranking $\rho$, the inequality $\rho(n)<\rho(n)$ is false. Hence no direct self-dependency is ordinal guarded.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Strict ordinal order is irreflexive.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Pure Loop Obstruction]
A singleton proof graph whose only dependency begins and ends at its unique node admits no strictly decreasing ordinal ranking.
\end{corollary}

The obstruction is absolute: choosing a larger ordinal cannot help because no ordinal is below itself.

\begin{theorem}[Acyclicity of Ranked Dependencies]
Let $E(a,b)$ mean that $a$ depends on $b$, and suppose $\rho(b)<\rho(a)$ whenever $E(a,b)$. For every $k>0$ and path
\[
n_0\,E\,n_1\,E\cdots E\,n_k,
\]
one has $n_0\ne n_k$. Thus there is no nonempty finite directed dependency cycle.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Transitivity along the path gives $\rho(n_k)<\rho(n_0)$. If $n_k=n_0$, this becomes $\rho(n_0)<\rho(n_0)$, contradicting irreflexivity.
\end{proof}

Well-foundedness also excludes infinite dependency chains. Therefore edgewise ordinal descent validates compressed well-founded arguments, not genuine circular justification. A cyclic proof theory must instead use a global trace condition, delayed modality, parity condition, or another criterion permitting recurrence while enforcing productivity.

\section{Diagonal self-reference}

Structural loops and semantic diagonalization must be distinguished. Consider a sound system with a sentence $G$ asserting its own unprovability.

\begin{theorem}[Diagonal Unprovability]
In a sound diagonal system, the Goedel sentence asserting ``I am not provable in this system'' is not provable in that system.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose the sentence $G$ were provable. By soundness, $G$ would be true. Its content says that $G$ is not provable, contradicting the supposition. Hence $G$ is unprovable.
\end{proof}

This theorem is semantic: it relies on the meaning of provability, diagonal self-description, and soundness. The pure-loop obstruction is structural and uses only irreflexivity of ordinal order. Neither should be mistaken for the other. Together they emphasize that self-description is not self-justification.

\section{Algorithms and certificate checking}

For finite graphs and effectively represented ranks, the definitions yield elementary validation procedures.

\begin{algorithm}[Guarded Proof-Graph Validator]
For each assumption node $n$, test $C_n\in\ctx_n$. For each implication node $n$ labelled by $A,B$ with child $m$, test
\[
C_n=A\to B,\quad \ctx_m=A::\ctx_n,\quad C_m=B,\quad \rho(m)<\rho(n).
\]
Accept exactly when every test succeeds.
\end{algorithm}

With canonical formulas, traversal costs $O(|V|+|E|)$ apart from context and rank comparisons. Natural-number ranks use ordinary integer comparison; ordinal notations add a cost depending on their encoded length.

\begin{proposition}[Validator Correctness]
If the Guarded Proof-Graph Validator accepts, every node's sequent is ordinarily derivable.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Acceptance is precisely local well-typedness plus guardedness, so Guarded Graph Soundness applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{algorithm}[Dependency-Cycle Diagnostic]
Run depth-first search with unvisited, active, and completed marks. If an edge reaches an active node, report the resulting directed cycle. Otherwise output a reverse topological ordering.
\end{algorithm}

The running time is $O(|V|+|E|)$. By the Acyclicity Theorem, any reported cycle proves that no edgewise decreasing ordinal rank exists.

\begin{proposition}[Finite Rank Construction]
Every finite directed acyclic dependency graph has a ranking $h:V\to\mathbb N$ satisfying $h(m)<h(n)$ whenever $n$ depends on $m$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Process nodes in reverse topological order. Give every sink height $0$, and set
\[
h(n)=1+\max\{h(m):n\text{ depends on }m\}
\]
for every nonsink. Each child then has smaller height.
\end{proof}

Thus, for finite graphs, acyclicity is both necessary and sufficient for an edgewise decreasing natural-number ranking. The height construction computes longest dependency-path length and minimizes the largest rank under this convention.

\section{Applications}

\subsection{Recursive security reductions}

A cryptographic reduction transforms an adversary against one claim into an adversary against another. Complex arguments may share subreductions or revisit protocol components. A rank can measure remaining rounds, unresolved game hops, oracle nesting, or a lexicographic tuple. Guarded Graph Soundness requires every recursive invocation to lower that measure. A reduction of a security claim $S$ to unchanged $S$ is rejected as a self-loop, while a reduction from $S(k)$ to $S(k-1)$ may be legitimate when a base case is supplied.

This discipline does not establish security alone: local reduction steps and quantitative losses require separate proofs. It prevents recursive organization from concealing circular dependence.

\subsection{Termination and recursive programs}

The analogy with recursion is exact. A recursive function calls itself on an argument of smaller rank; a guarded proof node invokes a child derivation of smaller rank. Well-foundedness converts each recursive specification into a finite computation or derivation. The equation $x=x$ illustrates the limitation of fixed-point slogans: it has every value as a fixed point and selects none. A proof node pointing only to itself likewise contributes no evidence.

\subsection{Proof compression}

Directed acyclic graphs can store a repeated subderivation once rather than duplicate it in a tree. Soundness justifies unfolding this representation. The unfolded tree may be exponentially larger, so graph validation can be substantially more economical without adding logical strength.

\subsection{Cyclic inductive reasoning}

Genuine cyclic systems often impose infinite-trace progress. The Acyclicity Theorem explains why: strict decrease on every edge collapses the method to acyclic graphs. To obtain cyclic compression, some edges must preserve rank while every infinite trace repeatedly encounters designated progress.

\section{Domain-theoretic perspective}

Order partial proof trees by information content, placing an unfinished leaf below every refinement. Directed families of finite approximants may have infinite trees as least upper bounds, suggesting a Scott-domain semantics. Completion alone, however, does not distinguish valid proofs from arbitrary infinite trees. The unfolding of a pure loop can exist as an infinite object while containing no assumption leaf and no finite justification.

A least-fixed-point semantics admits only objects generated from finite rule applications and well-founded approximation. A greatest-fixed-point semantics more naturally admits infinite behavior but needs an independent productivity, fairness, or trace condition. Ordinal guarding describes a robust least-fixed-point fragment: every dependency is scheduled after lower-ranked dependencies, so each path is well founded. It does not turn circularity into evidence.

\section{Limitations and future work}

The calculus contains only assumptions and implication introduction. Further finitary rules follow the same induction pattern, but infinitary rules, coinductive predicates, and semantic side conditions require additional work. The results are qualitative: they do not bound unfolding size or settle the complexity of comparing general ordinal notations.

\subsection{Algebraic-domain completion}

Finite partial trees ordered by refinement are conjectured to complete to an algebraic Scott domain whose compact elements are precisely finite partial trees. The key problem is to characterize an admissible least fixed point selected by ordinal approximation. The domain may contain every infinite tree even though only a subset denotes derivations.

\subsection{Trace conditions for genuine cycles}

For cyclic proofs of inductive predicates, validity may be equivalent to a well-ordered progress measure for which every infinite branch contains infinitely many strict decreases between regeneration points. Such a Buechi-style condition would permit recurrence while demanding recurring progress. Edgewise descent cannot do this because it excludes all cycles.

\subsection{Modal productive self-reference}

A ``later'' modality can require every recursive occurrence to appear beneath an observable constructor. Guarded recursive equations should then have unique productive solutions. The height-one identity and pure loop form boundary tests: the first exposes a rule constructor and reaches an assumption, while the second reveals nothing before recurring.

\subsection{Complexity of ordinal certificates}

For ranks in Cantor normal form below a fixed ordinal such as $\varepsilon_0$, checking local typing and descent may be polynomial in graph size and notation length. Finding a minimum-rank certificate appears related to longest-path optimization and may be harder than checking a supplied certificate.

\section{Conclusion}

Proof graphs support sharing and recursive organization, but validity cannot rest on appearance or local syntax alone. Strict ordinal descent supplies a transparent global certificate. Every locally correct guarded graph unfolds into ordinary natural deduction. The identity $P\to P$ is the smallest positive example: a rank-$1$ root discharges a rank-$0$ assumption leaf. It is hypothetical reasoning, not self-justification. At the negative boundary, no loop can have smaller rank than itself and no nonempty finite dependency cycle survives transitive descent.

Non-tree syntax is therefore safe when it abbreviates well-founded justification. Edgewise ordinal guarding does not create genuinely circular truths. Richer cyclic systems remain possible, but they must articulate recurring progress through traces, modalities, or admissible fixed points while rejecting the empty promise of a proof that cites only itself.

