# Guarded Circular Proofs: An Interactive Journey

A backward arrow is not automatically a fallacy. The decisive question is whether every dependency makes measurable progress. Begin with the smallest positive case.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The root derives $P 	o P$ by temporarily adding $P$ to its context. This is a two-node derivation, not a proof that assumes its own conclusion.

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the Guarded Graph Soundness Theorem</summary>

If every node obeys its local inference rule and every dependency child has strictly smaller ordinal rank, well-founded induction reconstructs an ordinary derivation at every node. The assumption case is immediate; implication introduction invokes the induction hypothesis at its lower-ranked child and discharges the temporary antecedent.

</details>

## Why a true circle fails

Try to rank three nodes around a loop. The requested inequalities compose to $ho(A)<ho(A)$.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

{{algorithm:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the finite acyclicity proof</summary>

Along $n_0 	o n_1 	o \cdots 	o n_k$, strict descent gives $ho(n_k)<ho(n_0)$. If the path returns to its start, substitution violates irreflexivity. Thus every nonempty finite ranked cycle is impossible.

</details>

## Constructing certificates

For finite acyclic graphs, longest dependency-path length supplies a canonical natural-number rank.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

The validator then checks local labels and every rank inequality.

{{algorithm:0}}

## Security reductions as descending arguments

Recursive cryptographic reductions are credible when each invocation lowers a well-founded parameter and eventually reaches a base case.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

<details><summary>Background: fixed points and productivity</summary>

The equation $x=x$ is a fixed-point equation but selects no value. Likewise, a proof node citing only itself supplies no evidence. Richer cyclic systems need recurring trace progress or a modal “later” guard, not bare self-reference. For background, see [well-founded relations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Well-founded_relation), [cyclic proofs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyclic_proof), and [domain theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domain_theory).

</details>

## Compression without new logical strength

A directed acyclic graph may share subderivations that an ordinary tree repeats many times. Unfolding can grow rapidly, even though checking the compact certificate remains linear in graph traversal size apart from label comparisons.

{{visualization:2}}

{{demo:0}}

The final boundary is precise: safe graph syntax can look recursive, but edgewise ordinal descent certifies only well-founded justification. Genuine cyclic reasoning requires a more global productivity criterion.

